1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,009
A moss on the wall.

2
00:00:02,009 --> 00:00:03,412
A fern in a pot.

3
00:00:03,412 --> 00:00:04,868
A pine on a hill.

4
00:00:04,868 --> 00:00:05,990
A mango tree.

5
00:00:06,390 --> 00:00:07,698
All four are plants.

6
00:00:07,698 --> 00:00:10,280
But are they really
the same kind of plant?

7
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:14,922
Diya walked through the college
garden and saw all four in one walk.

8
00:00:14,922 --> 00:00:18,803
She asked: who decides which
plants belong together, and how?

9
00:00:19,153 --> 00:00:22,363
Botanists have tried three
big ways to sort plants.

10
00:00:22,363 --> 00:00:25,188
Each one fixed a problem
in the one before.

11
00:00:25,688 --> 00:00:30,256
By the end, you will know all three,
plus the new tools botanists use today.

12
00:00:30,906 --> 00:00:33,370
First, what is in the plant kingdom today?

13
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:37,934
Earlier, fungi and some single celled
living things with cell walls were

14
00:00:37,934 --> 00:00:39,140
kept with plants.

15
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,271
Now they have their own kingdoms.

16
00:00:41,721 --> 00:00:46,835
Today the plant kingdom has five groups:
algae, bryophytes, pteridophytes,

17
00:00:46,835 --> 00:00:48,805
gymnosperms and angiosperms.

18
00:00:49,455 --> 00:00:52,544
The oldest way is called
an artificial system.

19
00:00:52,894 --> 00:00:56,975
It used a few features you can see
at once: the habit of the plant, the

20
00:00:56,975 --> 00:00:59,306
colour, number and shape of its leaves.

21
00:00:59,756 --> 00:01:02,707
Linnaeus sorted plants
mainly by the androecium.

22
00:01:02,707 --> 00:01:06,379
That is the male part of a
flower, the ring of stamens.

23
00:01:06,879 --> 00:01:07,870
Problem one.

24
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:12,286
Using only a few features split up
plants that are closely related.

25
00:01:13,036 --> 00:01:14,001
Problem two.

26
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:17,852
It gave the same weight to
leaves and stems as to flowers.

27
00:01:17,852 --> 00:01:21,475
But leaves and stems change
easily with the surroundings.

28
00:01:22,225 --> 00:01:26,681
The same plant grows big leaves in
the shade and small leaves in the sun.

29
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:28,593
Leaf size alone can fool you.

30
00:01:29,243 --> 00:01:31,920
So botanists moved to natural systems.

31
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:37,244
A natural system looks for how alike
plants really are, using many features,

32
00:01:37,244 --> 00:01:38,665
outside and inside.

33
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:44,171
Inside means the fine structure of cells,
the anatomy, how the embryo develops,

34
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:46,470
and the chemicals the plant makes.

35
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:51,877
George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker
gave a natural system for flowering

36
00:01:51,877 --> 00:01:52,777
plants.

37
00:01:53,221 --> 00:01:55,380
Then came phylogenetic systems.

38
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:58,319
Phylo means a tribe or a family line.

39
00:01:58,319 --> 00:02:02,442
These systems sort plants by
evolution, by who came from whom.

40
00:02:02,892 --> 00:02:07,434
Plants in the same group are taken to
share a common ancestor, like cousins

41
00:02:07,434 --> 00:02:08,813
in one family tree.

42
00:02:09,463 --> 00:02:11,820
Let's put the first two side by side.

43
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:16,123
Artificial: a few features,
often just leaves or stamens.

44
00:02:16,123 --> 00:02:19,705
Two unrelated plants with
similar leaves land together.

45
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:22,989
Natural: many features,
outside and inside.

46
00:02:22,989 --> 00:02:25,640
Plants that are truly alike land together.

47
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:27,160
Your turn.

48
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:31,133
A system groups plants only
by the shape of their leaves.

49
00:02:31,133 --> 00:02:32,750
Artificial or natural?

50
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:34,103
Pause and decide.

51
00:02:37,303 --> 00:02:38,203
Artificial!

52
00:02:38,188 --> 00:02:43,097
It uses one outside feature, and leaf
shape changes with the surroundings.

53
00:02:43,747 --> 00:02:45,035
Remember this.

54
00:02:45,035 --> 00:02:47,563
Artificial uses a few features.

55
00:02:47,563 --> 00:02:49,163
Natural uses many.

56
00:02:49,163 --> 00:02:51,305
Phylogenetic uses ancestry.

57
00:02:51,905 --> 00:02:55,079
Today, botanists also use three new tools.

58
00:02:55,429 --> 00:02:57,865
Numerical taxonomy uses computers.

59
00:02:57,865 --> 00:03:01,880
Every feature you can observe
gets a number or a code.

60
00:03:02,330 --> 00:03:06,832
Each feature gets equal importance, and
hundreds of features can be compared

61
00:03:06,832 --> 00:03:07,732
at once.

62
00:03:08,174 --> 00:03:12,544
Cytotaxonomy uses the cell: the
number of chromosomes, their structure

63
00:03:12,544 --> 00:03:14,040
and how they behave.

64
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:19,401
Chemotaxonomy uses the chemicals
inside the plant, to settle confusing

65
00:03:19,401 --> 00:03:20,301
cases.

66
00:03:20,734 --> 00:03:25,099
Now let's watch features sort our
four plants, one question at a time.

67
00:03:25,449 --> 00:03:26,413
Question one.

68
00:03:26,413 --> 00:03:29,817
Does the plant body have
true roots, stem and leaves?

69
00:03:29,817 --> 00:03:31,084
The moss says no.

70
00:03:31,084 --> 00:03:32,985
It drops out as a bryophyte.

71
00:03:33,435 --> 00:03:34,502
Question two.

72
00:03:34,502 --> 00:03:36,008
Does it make seeds?

73
00:03:36,008 --> 00:03:37,410
The fern says no.

74
00:03:37,410 --> 00:03:39,021
It is a pteridophyte.

75
00:03:39,471 --> 00:03:40,617
Question three.

76
00:03:40,617 --> 00:03:43,197
Are the seeds covered inside a fruit?

77
00:03:43,197 --> 00:03:44,569
The pine says no.

78
00:03:44,569 --> 00:03:46,790
Its seeds sit naked on a cone.

79
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:48,264
It is a gymnosperm.

80
00:03:48,714 --> 00:03:50,156
The mango says yes.

81
00:03:50,156 --> 00:03:54,950
Its seed is inside a fruit, so it
is an angiosperm, a flowering plant.

82
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,390
Now, a worked example.

83
00:03:57,390 --> 00:04:00,494
Which system or tool
is each botanist using?

84
00:04:00,844 --> 00:04:04,779
Botanist one groups plants by
the number of stamens alone.

85
00:04:04,779 --> 00:04:06,878
That is an artificial system.

86
00:04:07,328 --> 00:04:11,501
Botanist two counts the chromosomes
of two look alike plants.

87
00:04:11,501 --> 00:04:13,085
That is cytotaxonomy.

88
00:04:13,535 --> 00:04:18,146
Botanist three feeds two hundred
coded features into a computer.

89
00:04:18,146 --> 00:04:20,241
That is numerical taxonomy.

90
00:04:20,691 --> 00:04:24,291
Botanist four compares the
chemicals in their leaves.

91
00:04:24,291 --> 00:04:25,921
That is chemotaxonomy.

92
00:04:26,371 --> 00:04:29,955
Botanist five groups plants
by their common ancestor.

93
00:04:29,955 --> 00:04:32,129
That is a phylogenetic system.

94
00:04:32,779 --> 00:04:34,245
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:34,594 --> 00:04:39,057
Many students say numerical taxonomy
gives more weight to the important

96
00:04:39,057 --> 00:04:39,957
features.

97
00:04:40,254 --> 00:04:41,714
It does the opposite.

98
00:04:41,714 --> 00:04:46,029
Every feature gets equal importance,
and hundreds are compared together.

99
00:04:46,679 --> 00:04:48,131
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:48,131 --> 00:04:52,436
As match the following: each system
or tool paired with the information

101
00:04:52,436 --> 00:04:53,336
it uses.

102
00:04:53,786 --> 00:04:55,026
Exam level now.

103
00:04:55,026 --> 00:04:57,517
Match each term with what it uses.

104
00:04:57,517 --> 00:04:58,809
Pause and solve.

105
00:05:04,209 --> 00:05:05,585
The answer is B.

106
00:05:06,085 --> 00:05:09,902
Numerical taxonomy uses
all observable features.

107
00:05:09,902 --> 00:05:12,350
Cytotaxonomy uses chromosomes.

108
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:15,571
Chemotaxonomy uses chemical constituents.

109
00:05:16,071 --> 00:05:20,471
And the artificial system used a few
features, such as the androecium.

110
00:05:21,071 --> 00:05:22,530
Here is your recap.

111
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:24,030
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:30,228
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:05:30,628 --> 00:05:31,528
One.

114
00:05:31,193 --> 00:05:34,440
Why can leaf size fool
an artificial system?

115
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:36,240
Two.

116
00:05:35,855 --> 00:05:39,235
What does a natural system
look at inside a plant?

117
00:05:40,135 --> 00:05:41,035
Three.

118
00:05:40,753 --> 00:05:43,084
Which tool would count chromosomes?

119
00:05:44,184 --> 00:05:47,329
Next time: green slime
on the water tank wall.

120
00:05:47,329 --> 00:05:48,502
Is it a plant?

121
00:05:48,502 --> 00:05:50,079
We find out in algae.
